---
import { t, tArray } from '../utils/i18n';
import type { Locale } from '../utils/i18n';
import { withBase } from '../utils/url';
import EarthGlobe from './EarthGlobe.astro';
import Galaxy from './Galaxy.astro';

interface Props { locale: Locale }
const { locale } = Astro.props;

const stats = tArray<{ text: string; offline?: boolean }>(locale, 'classroom_stats');

/*
 * The hero shows the product, not an illustration of it: a live, draggable
 * 3D model from the library, re-framed in our own chrome (blue/yellow/green
 * dots instead of macOS traffic lights). The panel is left uncovered on
 * purpose — the model is the demo, and a second card cropping its corner only
 * competed with it.
 *
 * What the panel is NOT any more is a lesson in progress. It used to wear a
 * LIVE pill and a "18/22 completed" badge because a quiz ran inside it; the
 * client asked for the quiz out, and both of those were labels on the quiz
 * rather than on the model. The pill now names the thing on screen and the
 * badge says where it came from — which is the claim the band further down
 * the page then backs with 1200 more of them.
 *
 * The three floating objects next to the globe are subjects, not controls:
 * beside a live planet, a coin and an alarm clock read as a shop and a
 * deadline. Rocket and dinosaur are drawn for this site (sources in
 * src/assets/icon-src/) in the pack's own clay language — one saturated
 * body colour, cream detail, soft key light from the upper left — so they
 * sit next to the bought objects without looking imported. The in-lesson
 * tools keep the coin/clock/music trio down in the platform band, where
 * they carry their labels. The third object is the one that had to be drawn
 * in the page instead of exported, because it turns — see Galaxy.astro.
 */
const tools = [
  { key: 'rocket', title: t(locale, 'classroom_hero_space_title'), cls: 'tool-a' },
  { key: 'dino', title: t(locale, 'classroom_hero_dino_title'), cls: 'tool-b' },
  { key: 'galaxy', title: t(locale, 'classroom_hero_galaxy_title'), cls: 'tool-c' }
];
---

<section class="hero" id="top">
  <span class="blob blob-y" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="blob blob-g" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="blob blob-b" aria-hidden="true"></span>

  <div class="hero-grid wrap">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <span class="eyebrow enter" style="--d:0ms">{t(locale, 'classroom_eyebrow')}</span>
      <h1 class="enter" style="--d:60ms" set:html={t(locale, 'classroom_title')} />
      <p class="lede enter" style="--d:120ms">{t(locale, 'classroom_lede')}</p>
      <div class="hero-chips enter" style="--d:180ms">
        {stats.map((s) => (
          <span class={`chip${s.offline ? ' is-green' : ''}`}>
            <i class={`dot${s.offline ? ' pulse' : ''}`}></i>{s.text}
          </span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="hero-cta enter" style="--d:240ms">
        <a href="#demo" class="btn btn-primary">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_cta_primary')}</a>
        <a href="#platform" class="btn btn-ghost">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_cta_secondary')}</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="hero-stage">
      <figure class="shot is-hero enter" style="--d:80ms">
        <figcaption class="shot-bar">
          <i></i><i></i><i></i>
          <span class="shot-meta">
            <span class="shot-ttl">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_panel_title')}</span>
            {/* Not the shared `.shot-live` pill: that one is green with a
                pulsing dot because it means a session is running right now,
                which is exactly the claim this panel stopped making. The
                platform band still uses it, on a panel where it is true. */}
            <span class="shot-kind">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_panel_tag')}</span>
          </span>
        </figcaption>
        <EarthGlobe locale={locale} />
      </figure>

      {/* Where the model comes from, not how a class is doing with it. The
          mark is a cut-away in four bands — the badge draws the same idea the
          panel behind it is demonstrating, at 34px.

          The bands are hexagons rather than circles because this badge and
          the library's pickers are the same claim — "a 3D model out of the
          Eddy catalogue" — and that claim is the one thing on this site
          allowed to be a hexagon. Same geometry as --hex-mask: a pointy-top
          hexagon whose corners are rounded by a fat round-joined stroke, so
          every ring keeps the polygon at 78% of its width and spends the
          other 22% on the stroke. The removed wedge is one sixth exactly —
          the 60° sector between the top vertex and the next one round. */}
      <div class="badge badge-model enter" style="--d:420ms">
        <span class="badge-mark" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 32 36.95" width="29.4" height="34" focusable="false">
            <polygon class="m1" points="16,4.97 27.7,11.72 27.7,25.23 16,31.99 4.3,25.23 4.3,11.72" stroke-width="6.6" />
            <polygon class="m2" points="16,8.39 24.74,13.43 24.74,23.52 16,28.56 7.26,23.52 7.26,13.43" stroke-width="4.93" />
            <polygon class="m3" points="16,11.99 21.62,15.23 21.62,21.72 16,24.96 10.38,21.72 10.38,15.23" stroke-width="3.17" />
            <polygon class="m4" points="16,15.59 18.5,17.03 18.5,19.92 16,21.36 13.5,19.92 13.5,17.03" stroke-width="1.41" />
            {/* the wedge the model is cut on, painted in the badge's own
                white so it reads as removed rather than as a fifth colour.
                It overshoots the outer ring so the cut runs clean through the
                rounded corner as well — the overshoot lands on the badge's
                own white, so it is invisible. */}
            <path class="m0" d="M16 18.475 L16 1.18 L30.98 9.83 Z" />
          </svg>
        </span>
        <span class="txt">
          <span class="t1">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_model_source')}</span>
          <span class="t2">{t(locale, 'classroom_hero_model_count')}</span>
        </span>
      </div>

      {/* No loading="lazy" on these two: they are in the first viewport at
          every width, and a lazy image above the fold is fetched only after
          layout — it used to arrive after its own 500ms entrance cue. */}
      {/* Two elements per badge, for the reason Library3D.astro spells out:
          a mask clips away anything a filter draws outside it, so the shape
          lives on the inner element and the shadow on the outer one. */}
      {tools.map((tool, i) => (
        <div class={`badge badge-tool ${tool.cls} enter`} style={`--d:${500 + i * 80}ms`} title={tool.title}>
          <span class="tool-face">
            {tool.key === 'galaxy'
              ? <Galaxy />
              : <img class="obj is-inline" src={withBase(`/assets/icons/${tool.key}.png`)} width="256" height="256" alt="" decoding="async" />}
          </span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    /* Half a band at the bottom, not a full one. The scroll cue used to sit
       in that gap and carry it; with the cue gone, a full --pad-band here
       plus a full one on top of "why us" left the hero floating in a hole. */
    padding: calc(var(--nav-h) + clamp(32px, 5vw, 72px)) 0 clamp(28px, 3.4vw, 56px);
    /* Clip X, never Y. The old `overflow: hidden` guillotined the green and
       blue pools in a straight line across the full viewport width at the
       hero's bottom edge — the one visible seam on the page. But the yellow
       pool does hang 173px past the right edge, and body's `overflow-x`
       suppresses the scrollbar without stopping the document from measuring
       wider. `overflow-x: clip` is the one value that cuts a single axis:
       `hidden` would force the other axis to `auto` and re-cut the bleed. */
    overflow-x: clip;
  }

  /* Three soft blocks of brand colour instead of an atmospheric gradient —
     the mock builds depth out of flat shapes, so the page does too.

     They are the same three colours as the sheets in the header, and they
     are driven by the same two moments: they bloom in with the stack when
     the page loads, and green + blue drain away on the exact beat their
     sheets are pulled out of the header (`html.nav-stuck`, set in app.js).
     Yellow is the one that stays in both places. */
  .blob {
    position: absolute;
    /* Load-bearing: this drops the three pools into the same page-wide
       ambient plane as every other band (see style.css), so the hero's light
       and the next band's light are literally one layer and cannot show a
       join. */
    z-index: -1;
    border-radius: 50%;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* No filter: blur(). A radial falloff is already smooth; the blur was a
       second full-size GPU pass replayed on every nav-stuck toggle, for an
       effect the third colour stop below gives away for free. */
    transition:
      opacity 620ms var(--ease-out),
      transform 760ms var(--ease-out);
  }
  /* Offsets are calc'd off each pool's own size, not off the hero's height —
     a percentage offset would resolve against the section and drift with the
     copy length between UK and EN. The middle colour stop is what keeps a
     wash this wide from banding on an 8-bit panel. */
  /* The yellow is the widest thing on the page on purpose: it is where light
     enters the site, and a tight pool made it read as a corner highlight
     rather than as the room being lit. Wider ellipse, a longer plateau before
     the falloff starts, and its own fade past the shared 62% so the wash
     reaches the middle of the hero instead of dying at the shoulder. */
  .blob-y {
    --h: min(68vw, 1020px);
    width: var(--h); height: calc(var(--h) * .82);
    top: calc(var(--h) * -.26);
    right: calc(var(--h) * -.20);
    background: radial-gradient(ellipse,
      rgba(var(--amb-yellow), var(--amb-4)) 0%,
      rgba(var(--amb-yellow), calc(var(--amb-4) * .62)) 32%,
      rgba(var(--amb-yellow), calc(var(--amb-4) * .24)) 54%,
      rgba(var(--amb-yellow), 0) 76%);
  }

  /* Green and blue are centred all but exactly ON the hero's bottom edge, so
     half of each pool lands in the top padding of "why us" — precisely where
     the old clip used to cut them off. */
  .blob-g {
    --h: min(46vw, 620px);
    width: var(--h); height: var(--h);
    bottom: calc(var(--h) * -.46);
    left: calc(var(--h) * -.18);
    background: radial-gradient(circle,
      rgba(var(--amb-green), var(--amb-3)) 0%,
      rgba(var(--amb-green), calc(var(--amb-3) * .45)) 34%,
      rgba(var(--amb-green), 0) var(--amb-fade));
    transition-delay: 60ms;
  }
  .blob-b {
    --h: min(40vw, 520px);
    width: var(--h); height: var(--h);
    bottom: calc(var(--h) * -.50);
    right: 4%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle,
      rgba(var(--amb-blue), var(--amb-2)) 0%,
      rgba(var(--amb-blue), calc(var(--amb-2) * .45)) 34%,
      rgba(var(--amb-blue), 0) var(--amb-fade));
    transition-delay: 120ms;
  }

  /* load: the light comes up with the same stagger as the stack fanning open */
  html.js:not(.is-loaded) .blob { opacity: 0; transform: scale(.9); }

  /* stuck: green and blue leave — drifting the way their sheets do — and the
     yellow that remains opens out a little to take the room */
  html.nav-stuck .blob-g { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(7vw) scale(.94); transition-delay: 110ms; }
  html.nav-stuck .blob-b { opacity: 0; transform: translateX(7vw) scale(.94); transition-delay: 0ms; }
  html.nav-stuck .blob-y { transform: scale(1.07); }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .blob { transition: none; }
  }

  .hero-grid {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 46%) 1fr;
    gap: clamp(32px, 4vw, 64px);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .hero-copy h1 { margin: 20px 0 0; }
  .hero-copy .lede { margin-top: 20px; }

  .hero-chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px; margin-top: 28px; }
  .hero-cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 12px; margin-top: 32px; }

  /* The window chrome is a fixed 42px strip (style.css), so anything in it
     that wraps spills straight over the model. On a 390px phone the title and
     the pill together are wider than the bar, and the shared rules have no
     opinion about which of them should give — so the scoped rules here say
     it: the pill is short and keeps its full width, and the title, which is
     scene-setting rather than information, gives ground and then ellipses.
     A `min-width: 0` on the flex row is what lets it shrink at all. */
  .shot-bar .shot-meta { min-width: 0; }
  .shot-bar .shot-ttl {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
  }

  /* What kind of thing is open in the window. Same pill geometry as the
     shared `.shot-live`, deliberately not the same colour: green with a
     heartbeat means "happening now", and this is a model sitting still until
     somebody touches it. */
  .shot-kind {
    flex: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    color: var(--ink-2);
    border-radius: var(--r-pill);
    padding: 3px 11px;
    font-size: .6875rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: .05em;
  }

  /* ---- stage ---- */
  .hero-stage { position: relative; }
  .badge {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 5;
    background: #fff;
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--e3);
  }
  .badge-model {
    top: 10%;
    left: -10%;
    padding: 10px 16px 10px 11px;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 11px;
  }
  .badge-mark { display: block; flex: none; line-height: 0; }
  /* every ring strokes itself: the stroke IS the corner rounding, so it has
     to carry the ring's own colour rather than sit on top of it */
  .badge-mark polygon { stroke: inherit; stroke-linejoin: round; }
  .badge-mark .m1 { fill: var(--blue); stroke: var(--blue); }
  .badge-mark .m2 { fill: var(--yellow); stroke: var(--yellow); }
  .badge-mark .m3 { fill: var(--green); stroke: var(--green); }
  .badge-mark .m4 { fill: var(--ink); stroke: var(--ink); }
  .badge-mark .m0 { fill: #fff; }
  .badge-model .txt { display: flex; flex-direction: column; line-height: 1.3; }
  .badge-model .t1 { font-family: var(--font-display); font-weight: 800; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--ink); }
  .badge-model .t2 { font-size: 11.5px; font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink-3); font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  /* The three subjects are hexagons for the same reason the source badge is:
     each one is an object out of the library. They give up the shared
     `.badge` plate to get there — white surface and rounded corners come
     from the mask now, and the shadow is a drop-shadow on this outer box
     because a box-shadow would be drawn on a plate that is no longer there. */
  .badge-tool {
    width: 74px;
    height: calc(74px * var(--hex-ratio));
    right: -18px;
    padding: 0;
    background: none;
    border-radius: 0;
    box-shadow: none;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 6px 14px rgba(15, 18, 45, .18));
  }
  .tool-face {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    background: #fff;
    -webkit-mask-image: var(--hex-mask);
    mask-image: var(--hex-mask);
    -webkit-mask-size: 100% 100%;
    mask-size: 100% 100%;
    -webkit-mask-repeat: no-repeat;
    mask-repeat: no-repeat;
  }
  /* a shade smaller than the old 54px: a hexagon narrows towards its point,
     so an object sized to the full width would clip its own top corners */
  .badge-tool .obj { width: 50px; height: 50px; }
  /* the galaxy is drawn by its own component, so it carries its own size —
     :global is the only way to bring it down to the same 50px */
  .badge-tool :global(.gx) { width: 50px; height: 50px; }
  .tool-a { top: 70px; }
  .tool-b { top: 250px; }
  .tool-c { top: 430px; }

  /* ambient bob — deliberately non-harmonic periods so they never sync */
  .badge-model { animation: bob 5.2s var(--ease-in-out) infinite; }
  .tool-a { animation: bob 6.1s var(--ease-in-out) infinite; }
  .tool-b { animation: bob 7s var(--ease-in-out) infinite; }
  .tool-c { animation: bob 6.6s var(--ease-in-out) infinite; }
  @keyframes bob { 0%, 100% { translate: 0 0; } 50% { translate: 0 -5px; } }

  /* ---- entrance ---- */
  :global(.js) .enter { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(18px); }
  :global(.is-loaded) .enter {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: none;
    transition: opacity 520ms var(--ease-out) var(--d, 0ms), transform 520ms var(--ease-out) var(--d, 0ms);
  }
  :global(.is-loaded) .shot.enter { transition-duration: var(--t-enter); }
  :global(.js) .badge.enter { transform: translateY(18px) scale(.86); }
  :global(.is-loaded) .badge.enter {
    transition: opacity 520ms var(--ease-out) var(--d), transform 520ms var(--ease-spring) var(--d);
  }

  /* ---- stacked: the chips move under the demo ----
     On one column the two chips sat between the lede and the buttons, i.e.
     directly on the path from the sentence that explains the product to the
     button that asks for the sale — two yellow-and-green pills interrupting
     the one moment the page has to close. Under the demo panel they do the
     job they were written for instead: the reader has just turned the model,
     and "1–12 класи / працює офлайн" is the caption to what they touched.

     The reorder is done by making .hero-copy `display: contents`, so its
     children become items of the hero's own flex column and can be ordered
     freely — rather than by rendering the chips twice and hiding one, which
     is two sources of truth for one sentence. Each child then carries its own
     top margin, which is why the column's gap is zero. */
  @media (max-width: 1080px) {
    .hero-grid {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .hero-copy { display: contents; }
    .hero-copy .eyebrow { order: 1; align-self: center; }
    .hero-copy h1 { order: 2; margin-inline: auto; }
    .hero-copy .lede { order: 3; margin-inline: auto; }
    .hero-cta { order: 4; justify-content: center; margin-top: 32px; }
    .hero-stage { order: 5; max-width: 620px; margin: 44px auto 0; width: 100%; }
    .hero-chips { order: 6; justify-content: center; margin-top: 26px; }
    .badge-model { left: -4%; }
    .badge-tool { right: -10px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .badge-model { top: 4%; left: -2%; padding: 8px 12px 8px 9px; }
    .badge-model .t1 { font-size: 12px; }
    .badge-tool { width: 58px; height: calc(58px * var(--hex-ratio)); right: -8px; padding: 0; }
    .badge-tool .obj { width: 39px; height: 39px; }
    .badge-tool :global(.gx) { width: 39px; height: 39px; }
    .tool-a { top: 40px; }
    .tool-b { top: 172px; }
    .tool-c { top: 304px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .tool-c { display: none; }
  }
</style>
